# Feature, as the owner chose it on 27 Oct 2026: Propose, Discuss, Approve plan, Walk, Refine, Deliver.
# The walk comes before any test, and Fleet places each step from what it saw and nudges once if a
# test is written before the walk is approved. `.claude/decisions/2026-10-27-a-feature-job-walks-before-it-tests.md`
# has the reasoning; `crates/core-model/domain/workflowdef-fields.toml` has `ends_on` and `walks`.
# `bug.json` beside this file carries what all of them hold to. This file is also what Armada ships.
#
# **`propose` ends on an approval, never on a reply** (28 Oct 2026): the owner reads a sketch or a walk,
# not prose. A picked option counts only where the agent's question gave it a preview.
#
# **`deliver` is gated `human_always`.** New behaviour is the case where the engineer looks every
# time (`workflows.toml` has the reason). The owner decided on 19 Oct 2026 that Auto land does not stop
# there; Fleet declares the gate and does not enforce it, and the step's instructions carry the stop.
#
# **`asks_delivery` is on `propose`**, the first step: the start card's answer is carried only on the
# first turn, so a later step would lose it.
#
# **`implements_the_scope` tolerates an unscoped restructure, and its sibling is why it can.** Its
# criterion once refused a Job that split a module at the 900-line refusal while
# `no_behaviour_beyond_scope` had answered `met` (#190). The two must not converge: narrowing this one
# to behaviour makes it a second spelling of its sibling.
{
  "version": 1,
  "workflow_id": "feature",
  "name": "feature",
  "for_requests": "Adding, removing or changing what the software does or shows: a new screen, option or command, a change to how something behaves, or taking away something people could see or use, meant to land and stay. Fixing something broken is a bug, and moving code around while nothing anyone sees changes is a refactor.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "propose",
      "label": "Propose",
      "about": "Shows what it took the request to mean as a sketch, or a rough walk for a look, with two lines at most, then stops until you approve it.",
      "instructions": "Show what you took the request to mean and a first idea as a sketch with show_sketch, or a rough walk with show_window where the idea is about how something looks, with at most two lines of text beside it. No paragraphs, no lists of options in prose. Read only what that needs. Then stop and wait for the person to approve it. Where there are several ideas, ask which with AskUserQuestion and give each option a preview.",
      "ends_on": ["walk_approved", "sketch_approved", "draft_approved", "idea_picked"],
      "asks_delivery": true,
      "delivers": false,
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "discuss",
      "label": "Discuss",
      "about": "Settles the scope with you, with sketches and ideas, and a rough walk when the idea is about the look. Nothing on the branch changes but a mock.",
      "instructions": "Ask what is unclear, one question at a time. Show ideas as sketches. Where the idea is about how something looks, a rough walk may come now. Edit nothing on the branch but a mock.\nPut what the code and the request leave open to the person with ask_the_person. Read the request and every link in it, and the code it touches.\nSeparate what already exists from what is new: for each piece say done, partial or missing, with a path. Say partial when it is partial and which half is missing.\nIf he says he is not ready for a plan, keep talking and record no plan until he asks for one.",
      "delivers": false,
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "approve_plan",
      "label": "Approve plan",
      "about": "Writes down the change as tasks, with a walk named for each piece you would see. Nothing is changed yet. The tasks are what Refine works from.",
      "instructions": "Record the plan: what the person gets, in his words, the groups in order, and a walk for each piece he would see. Wait for approval.\nSay `seen: true` in record_plan when the person would see any piece of the change.\nList the tasks in the order they must happen. Tests come after the walk, so name none to write yet.\nName the files you expect to touch.\nOpen each function you plan to call and read its signature before writing it down. If an assumption from exploration was wrong, say so in the plan.\nSay when the work is bigger than it looked and which part grew.\nEnd with what you could not settle, each with file:line evidence.\nRecord the plan with record_plan, with a summary of a few lines in the person's own words saying what he gets (no file names or types) and a gives line per group, and wait for the person to approve it. Edit nothing before then but a mock.",
      "evidence": {
        "submitted": {
          "type": "plan"
        }
      },
      "mechanical_checks": [
        {
          "type": "plan_recorded",
          "min_tasks": 1
        }
      ],
      "evidence_scope": {
        "context_source": "drone_declared"
      },
      "judge_checks": [
        {
          "enabled": true,
          "model": "haiku",
          "criteria": [
            {
              "criterion_id": "addresses_the_request",
              "question": "Does this scope note address what was actually requested, without adding a feature or refactor nobody asked for? Fixing prose the change makes wrong, tests that prove the change, and files the change regenerates are part of addressing the request, not an expansion of it."
            },
            {
              "criterion_id": "names_what_it_will_touch",
              "question": "Does this scope note name the specific files, modules or behaviours the change will touch, rather than restating the request in other words?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "delivers": false,
      "advance_gate": "auto_if_judge_passes"
    },
    {
      "id": "walk",
      "label": "Walk",
      "about": "Builds the mock in the real Bridge on fixtures and shows it to you as a walk. No tests until you approve it. Passed over when nothing would be seen.",
      "instructions": "Build the mock in the real Bridge on fixtures and show it as a walk. Write no tests until it is approved. Notes come back as another take.\nShow it with show_window and wait for the person's approval. A change nobody would see in Bridge needs no walk.",
      "walks": true,
      "delivers": false,
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "refine",
      "label": "Refine",
      "about": "Wires it for real and writes the tests, from the plan, then runs the repository's checks on the result.",
      "instructions": "Wire it to the real thing and write the tests now. Show a new walk only if the look changed.\nWork the plan's tasks in order and finish each one.\nFollow the plan. A small correction, such as a signature that differs or a helper that already exists, is yours to make and to say. A change of approach goes to the person with ask_the_person.\nMatch the neighbouring code: naming, comment density, how errors are built, how tests are named.\nEvery new test is shown failing without the change, for the right reason, then passing.\nRun the repository's checks before you report done. A failed check hands back its ids and log tails.\nCommit in coherent pieces with messages that say why. Review reads only what is committed.",
      "evidence": {
        "submitted": {
          "type": "diff"
        },
        "captured": true
      },
      "retry_limit": 2,
      "follows_plan": true,
      "mechanical_checks": [
        {
          "type": "every_manifest_check"
        },
        {
          "type": "diff_nonempty"
        }
      ],
      "declare_plan_at": "step_start",
      "evidence_scope": {
        "context_source": "drone_declared",
        "reference_docs": [
          "plan.evidence"
        ],
        "scope_diff_check": true
      },
      "judge_checks": [
        {
          "enabled": true,
          "model": "haiku",
          "criteria": [
            {
              "criterion_id": "implements_the_scope",
              "question": "Does this diff implement what the scope note described, rather than something else in its place? Unscoped work that only restructures -- a move, a rename, a file split that leaves behaviour where it was -- is not a refusal on this criterion."
            },
            {
              "criterion_id": "no_behaviour_beyond_scope",
              "question": "Does this diff change behaviour beyond what the scope note described?"
            },
            {
              "criterion_id": "tasks_match_the_diff",
              "question": "Does the diff do every task set to done or handed in, and does each dropped task's reason hold?"
            },
            {
              "criterion_id": "the_evidence_accounts_for_itself",
              "question": "For each task the plan records as done or handed in, does what it recorded under `shown` either demonstrate what that task recorded under `expects`, or say why the work proved it another way? A task that proved its work differently and says so is not a refusal on this criterion -- what is refused is a `shown` that neither matches nor accounts for itself. A task with no `expects` recorded, and a task neither done nor handed in, are both outside this question."
            },
            {
              "criterion_id": "tests_exercise_behaviour",
              "question": "Do the tests in this diff exercise the behaviour the scope note described, rather than restating the implementation?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "delivers": false,
      "advance_gate": "auto_if_judge_passes"
    },
    {
      "id": "deliver",
      "label": "Deliver",
      "about": "Delivers the finished change the way you chose: merged after ci, opened as a pull request, or kept on the branch.",
      "instructions": "Open the pull request and merge it on a green ci. Say what landed.\nDeliver by the delivery the person chose. Fleet told you the answer when it was given, or the first message named it. Do not ask again.\nReview the branch against the task with git log and git diff against the base. If anything blocks, put every blocker in one ask_the_person question and stop.\nLeave the branch landable: git status is empty, nothing the repository ignores is committed, noise commits such as wip or fix typo are squashed, and messages say why.\nAuto land: push the branch, open the pull request, watch ci until it finishes and merge when it is green. A red ci is fixed on the branch, never merged over.\nOpen PR only: push the branch and open the pull request. Stop there and do not merge.\nKeep local: stop at the commit on the Job's branch. Push nothing and open no pull request.\nReport the branch, what is on it, and what the person needs before merging: a follow-up not done, a decision they might reverse, a check that is green only because something unrelated was fixed.",
      "evidence": {
        "submitted": {
          "type": "review"
        }
      },
      "delivers": true,
      "advance_gate": "human_always"
    }
  ]
}
